    {
        "content": "<p>As a general rule of thumb, if you're writing a super long definition or theorem, splitting it into smaller chunks will not only be more readable, but it also tends to help think about the problem in byte-sized chunks which helps complete it. This is similar to how generalizing a theorem might (counterintuitively) help prove it.<br>\nFor example as you can see, <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=finSigmaFinEquiv#doc\">docs#finSigmaFinEquiv</a> is built from smaller <code>Equiv</code>s, such as <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Equiv.sumSigmaDistrib#doc\">docs#Equiv.sumSigmaDistrib</a>.</p>",
